item.text:
I expanded the chapter on the missing areas the reviewer flagged and added claim-level units for the 2023 economy record. I could not update `research-ledger-manifest.json` directly because this workspace is read-only, so I am flagging that as a tooling limit rather than silently omitting it.

**What improved**
- Added independent, claim-level evidence for:
  - 2023 macro performance and fragile rebound
  - property-sector and local-government debt risks
  - public investment and fiscal execution
  - household income and urban-rural distribution
  - personnel changes in the economic team
  - corruption/capture signals in the financial and state sector
  - official audit findings on hidden debt, weak accounting, and misallocated spending
- The chapter is still not fully saturated on project-level welfare effects, but the key gap set is materially tighter now.

**What remains weak**
- 5B.2 personnel quality and merit-based retention are still only partially supported.
- 5B.6 project-level public-investment outcomes remain mostly at the system/fiscal level, not project-by-project.
- 5B.8 distribution across regions/subgroups still needs more targeted household or labor market work.
- 5B.10 durability of the trajectory is still best described as a fragile rebound, not a durable structural turn.

**Updated evidence units**

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2023 Article IV Consultation with the People's Republic of China","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2024-02-02","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2024/02/01/pr2433-china-imf-executive-board-concludes-2023-article-iv-consultation","claim":"China's economy rebounded in 2023 in line with the authorities' target of around 5 percent, driven by domestic demand and supported by easing monetary policy, tax relief, and fiscal spending; the IMF also warned that property-sector weakness, local-government fiscal strains, weak productivity, and aging would continue to weigh on medium-term growth.","locator":"Lines 204-220 of the IMF press release, especially lines 205-218.","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-002","canonical_fact_key":"imf_2023_rebound_and_risks","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":[],"source_type":"multilateral_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF consultation conclusion with explicit 2023 outcome and policy-risk assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target_period_current","ruler_attribution":"strong_indirect","contrary_evidence":"The same IMF text says the property adjustment, local government debt, and weak productivity will continue to suppress growth and confidence.","underlying_fact_key":"china_2023_macroeconomic_rebound_and_imbalances","lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

